{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And his spirit was troubled.</b> Even though it is written regarding Nebuchadnezzar, 'and he said to the magicians: a dream I have dreamed, and my spirit is troubled (ותתפעם)' — that is because from the time the magicians came before him there was only one beating (פעימה), for had he told them the dream, it would surely be known to them what the interpretation would be. What Pharaoh did not forget was only the interpretation — this is what is meant: because he did not inquire except after interpreters, the chief cupbearer remembered Joseph." "<b>And it beat.</b> There is a difference between ותפעם and ותתפעם, for ותפעם cannot be understood as one who acts but does not actually act, whereas ותתפעם can be understood as explicitly one who makes himself as though his spirit is troubled but it is not troubled. And so every hitpa'el form, such as 'והתברך בלבבו' (and blesses himself in his heart), and similarly 'lie upon your bed and התחל' (feign), and many like them. Therefore one may interpret 'ותתפעם רוחו' of Nebuchadnezzar as meaning: he said, if I reveal the dream, I know that I will find many interpreters, and even so the truth will not be clear to me — whether it is its interpretation or not. Rather, I will conceal the dream, and whoever can reveal it, it is revealed and known that it is from HaShem, and His prophet is true, and whatever he interprets regarding my dream will come true — and so it is stated in the Yalmudenu. But Pharaoh, who revealed the dream, was foolish in the matter, except that he did not wish to kill his sages and magicians as Nebuchadnezzar did." "<b>And Pharaoh told them his dream.</b> What Nebuchadnezzar needed — to forget the dream — was more than Pharaoh, because the interpretation of Nebuchadnezzar's dream was for a long time and for the distant future; he would not believe Daniel regarding the interpretation, and Daniel would not be elevated through it, since the interpretation would not be fulfilled in his days. But when he informed them of the dream and the magicians could not tell it, he believed in it and also regarding the interpretation. But here, in Pharaoh's eyes, it was easy to believe the interpretation, for he said, 'כי נכון הדבר מעם האלקים וממהר האלקים לעשותו' (for the thing is established from Elohim, and Elohim hastens to do it)." "<b>And there was none who interpreted them.</b> The dreams — they thought they were two dreams."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {1 "Resolved והתחל as the hitpa'el form of the root חול (to feign/begin), reading it as a parallel example of the reflexive-pretense pattern."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:18.210636116Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}